1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,708
Imagine a sound you cannot escape.

2
00:00:02,708 --> 00:00:07,318
A low, steady rumble, like a truck idling somewhere down the road.

3
00:00:07,318 --> 00:00:08,505
You step outside.

4
00:00:08,505 --> 00:00:09,064
Nothing.

5
00:00:09,064 --> 00:00:10,112
You drive away.

6
00:00:10,112 --> 00:00:10,880
It follows.

7
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:14,233
And the person next to you hears nothing at all.

8
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:16,128
This is the Taos Hum.

9
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:18,645
Hundreds of people have described it.

10
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:24,086
Scientists spent a week hunting for it with the best instruments in the country.

11
00:00:24,086 --> 00:00:28,100
And the answer they found is stranger than any alien story.

12
00:00:28,566 --> 00:00:31,391
Tonight we run it as a listening survey.

13
00:00:31,391 --> 00:00:32,379
Five stations.

14
00:00:32,379 --> 00:00:35,133
Every claim gets measured, then scored.

15
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,205
Station one, the town.

16
00:00:37,205 --> 00:00:39,321
Station two, the instruments.

17
00:00:39,321 --> 00:00:41,510
Three, the people who hear it.

18
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:42,896
Four, the suspects.

19
00:00:42,896 --> 00:00:44,866
Five, the real explanation.

20
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:46,925
The question is simple.

21
00:00:46,925 --> 00:00:52,600
Is the hum a secret machine, a signal from something else, or something inside us?

22
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:53,499
Let's listen.

23
00:00:57,966 --> 00:00:58,903
Station one.

24
00:00:58,903 --> 00:00:59,606
The town.

25
00:00:59,606 --> 00:01:05,854
Taos, New Mexico, a high desert town of artists, adobe walls and mountain light.

26
00:01:05,854 --> 00:01:08,666
Not a place anyone would call noisy.

27
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:15,357
In the spring of nineteen ninety-one, residents began to complain of a low droning sound.

28
00:01:15,357 --> 00:01:16,476
Not every night.

29
00:01:16,476 --> 00:01:17,665
Not every person.

30
00:01:17,665 --> 00:01:19,833
But enough to get talked about.

31
00:01:20,299 --> 00:01:24,083
They described it as a diesel engine idling far away.

32
00:01:24,083 --> 00:01:26,082
Some felt it in their chest.

33
00:01:26,082 --> 00:01:30,366
Some said it made them dizzy, or kept them awake for months.

34
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:33,223
Local papers picked up the story.

35
00:01:33,223 --> 00:01:35,107
Neighbours compared notes.

36
00:01:35,107 --> 00:01:41,700
A pattern emerged: the sound was louder indoors, louder at night, and worse in quiet rooms.

37
00:01:42,166 --> 00:01:45,522
Some people had heard hums elsewhere before.

38
00:01:45,522 --> 00:01:46,895
Hueytown, Alabama.

39
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:48,116
Kokomo, Indiana.

40
00:01:48,116 --> 00:01:51,167
Even England, in the nineteen seventies.

41
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:54,599
But Taos was the first to make national news.

42
00:01:55,066 --> 00:01:59,131
By nineteen ninety-three, the complaints reached Washington.

43
00:01:59,131 --> 00:02:05,500
Members of Congress asked for an official investigation into a sound that nobody could record.

44
00:02:05,966 --> 00:02:07,705
The request was granted.

45
00:02:07,705 --> 00:02:12,633
A real, funded, scientific hunt for the Taos Hum was about to begin.

46
00:02:13,099 --> 00:02:23,233
Teams from the University of New Mexico, Sandia National Laboratories, Los Alamos and an Air Force research lab arrived in Taos that summer.

47
00:02:23,699 --> 00:02:29,782
Their plan: surround the town with every instrument that could hear, feel or detect a signal.

48
00:02:29,782 --> 00:02:32,333
If the hum existed, they would find it.

49
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:33,633
Station two.

50
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:34,744
The instruments.

51
00:02:34,744 --> 00:02:36,064
First, microphones.

52
00:02:36,064 --> 00:02:44,400
Sensitive enough to pick up sounds far below the quietest whisper, set up in the homes of people who said they heard it.

53
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:49,552
The signal they were hunting would sit between thirty and eighty hertz.

54
00:02:49,552 --> 00:02:54,833
That is deep bass, the range of a distant engine or a big speaker's lowest note.

55
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:01,061
Second, geophones, buried in the ground, to catch vibrations travelling through rock.

56
00:03:01,061 --> 00:03:05,400
If the hum came from underground machinery, these would feel it.

57
00:03:05,866 --> 00:03:13,424
Third, electromagnetic detectors, in case it was a radio signal, a power line, or something transmitting.

58
00:03:13,424 --> 00:03:15,800
They swept every band they could.

59
00:03:16,266 --> 00:03:18,477
A week of nights in the field.

60
00:03:18,477 --> 00:03:22,750
Teams working in shifts, in homes, in yards, on hillsides.

61
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:24,666
Hundreds of hours of data.

62
00:03:25,133 --> 00:03:26,627
The results came back.

63
00:03:26,627 --> 00:03:29,683
No sound at the frequencies people described.

64
00:03:29,683 --> 00:03:36,066
Nothing above the background noise, even with a detector able to see far below normal hearing.

65
00:03:36,533 --> 00:03:40,022
Seismic sensors recorded no unusual vibration.

66
00:03:40,022 --> 00:03:43,662
Electromagnetic sweeps found no matching signal.

67
00:03:43,662 --> 00:03:45,938
The instruments heard nothing.

68
00:03:45,938 --> 00:03:47,000
Not a whisper.

69
00:03:47,466 --> 00:03:52,140
The geophones did catch something once: a gopher, digging nearby.

70
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:56,166
That was the most dramatic discovery of the field study.

71
00:03:56,633 --> 00:04:00,367
The team published their findings in a scientific journal.

72
00:04:00,367 --> 00:04:06,033
Their conclusion was careful: no source found, but the people reporting it were sincere.

73
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:10,142
So if the machines heard nothing, who was hearing something?

74
00:04:10,142 --> 00:04:13,300
That is the part of the story that gets interesting.

75
00:04:13,766 --> 00:04:14,673
Station three.

76
00:04:14,673 --> 00:04:15,451
The hearers.

77
00:04:15,451 --> 00:04:22,966
The researchers mailed out a survey, eight thousand forms, to find out who was hearing the hum, and what they heard.

78
00:04:23,433 --> 00:04:26,033
About fourteen hundred people answered.

79
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:29,233
A hundred and sixty one said they heard the hum.

80
00:04:29,233 --> 00:04:32,233
That is about two percent of the respondents.

81
00:04:32,699 --> 00:04:36,747
Most hearers were between thirty and fifty-nine years old.

82
00:04:36,747 --> 00:04:38,841
Most said they heard it daily.

83
00:04:38,841 --> 00:04:43,866
Nearly three quarters described the same thing: an idling diesel engine.

84
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:45,264
One surprise.

85
00:04:45,264 --> 00:04:49,491
About one in five heard it more than fifty miles from Taos.

86
00:04:49,491 --> 00:04:50,852
It was not a place.

87
00:04:50,852 --> 00:04:52,500
It followed the person.

88
00:04:52,966 --> 00:04:54,537
Then came a clever test.

89
00:04:54,537 --> 00:04:59,970
Hearers listened to a tone generator and tuned it until it matched what they heard.

90
00:04:59,970 --> 00:05:03,766
The matches clustered between thirty-two and eighty hertz.

91
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:09,613
The sound also pulsed, slowly, between half a beat and two beats every second.

92
00:05:09,613 --> 00:05:14,166
A slow throb, like breathing, or a heartbeat, or a distant engine.

93
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:17,884
Some hearers said the hum drove them to despair.

94
00:05:17,884 --> 00:05:19,036
Some moved house.

95
00:05:19,036 --> 00:05:23,033
A few said it was the worst thing they had ever lived with.

96
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:26,750
Stories spread that dogs reacted to it too.

97
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:29,622
We could find no solid record of that.

98
00:05:29,622 --> 00:05:33,100
It remains an unverified legend, not evidence.

99
00:05:33,566 --> 00:05:36,463
Meanwhile, doctors noticed something else.

100
00:05:36,463 --> 00:05:38,739
Many hearers had no hearing loss.

101
00:05:38,739 --> 00:05:40,533
Some had trouble sleeping.

102
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:42,533
Some were under heavy stress.

103
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,569
A sound that is real to the person, but invisible to the machines.

104
00:05:47,569 --> 00:05:48,815
Hold that thought.

105
00:05:48,815 --> 00:05:50,199
Now to the suspects.

106
00:05:50,666 --> 00:05:51,621
Station four.

107
00:05:51,621 --> 00:05:52,576
The suspects.

108
00:05:52,576 --> 00:05:54,633
Every theory gets a line-up.

109
00:05:54,633 --> 00:05:58,233
Evidence in one column, imagination in the other.

110
00:05:58,699 --> 00:05:59,591
Suspect one.

111
00:05:59,591 --> 00:06:00,558
The military.

112
00:06:00,558 --> 00:06:08,733
The favourite theory said the hum came from very low frequency radio that the navy uses to talk to submarines.

113
00:06:09,199 --> 00:06:10,809
The idea sounds neat.

114
00:06:10,809 --> 00:06:13,798
But those signals are radio, not sound.

115
00:06:13,798 --> 00:06:21,233
Radio waves cannot be heard by human ears, and the survey found no matching radio signal in Taos.

116
00:06:21,699 --> 00:06:22,553
Suspect two.

117
00:06:22,553 --> 00:06:24,901
HAARP, the Alaska research array.

118
00:06:24,901 --> 00:06:26,893
People love it as a culprit.

119
00:06:26,893 --> 00:06:33,366
But it sits more than three thousand miles away, and its output does not make audible bass.

120
00:06:33,833 --> 00:06:34,951
Suspect three.

121
00:06:34,951 --> 00:06:35,669
Industry.

122
00:06:35,669 --> 00:06:40,619
Factories, pipelines, pumps and power plants do make low hums.

123
00:06:40,619 --> 00:06:46,766
In Kokomo, Indiana, investigators found real machines behind some complaints.

124
00:06:47,233 --> 00:06:49,001
Taos has no big factory.

125
00:06:49,001 --> 00:06:58,800
But low frequency sound travels very far, and a distant compressor can sound like nothing at all to a microphone, yet loud in a room.

126
00:06:59,266 --> 00:07:00,126
Suspect four.

127
00:07:00,126 --> 00:07:02,044
The one you were waiting for.

128
00:07:02,044 --> 00:07:07,599
An alien base, a hidden underground facility, a craft humming beneath the mountains.

129
00:07:08,066 --> 00:07:08,852
We checked.

130
00:07:08,852 --> 00:07:12,638
No record, no photograph, no measurement, no witness.

131
00:07:12,638 --> 00:07:16,638
It is a story, told because the hum has no known source.

132
00:07:16,638 --> 00:07:17,566
Nothing more.

133
00:07:18,033 --> 00:07:19,988
And think about the logic.

134
00:07:19,988 --> 00:07:24,049
A secret alien base would be heard by everyone nearby.

135
00:07:24,049 --> 00:07:25,253
Not two percent.

136
00:07:25,253 --> 00:07:26,456
Not a few dozen.

137
00:07:26,456 --> 00:07:27,133
Everyone.

138
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:28,541
Suspect five.

139
00:07:28,541 --> 00:07:30,063
The human ear itself.

140
00:07:30,063 --> 00:07:32,164
And this one is not a theory.

141
00:07:32,164 --> 00:07:36,366
It is a medical fact that the ear can make its own sounds.

142
00:07:36,833 --> 00:07:38,768
Tinnitus is the best known.

143
00:07:38,768 --> 00:07:42,711
A ringing, or a hum, or a buzz, with no outside source.

144
00:07:42,711 --> 00:07:47,300
Millions of people live with it, and it can sound like anything.

145
00:07:47,766 --> 00:07:54,449
There are also spontaneous otoacoustic emissions: tiny sounds the inner ear itself produces.

146
00:07:54,449 --> 00:07:58,300
In a quiet room at night, some people can sense them.

147
00:07:58,766 --> 00:08:01,257
But the ear cannot explain everything.

148
00:08:01,257 --> 00:08:05,191
Others heard it far from home, and some had perfect hearing.

149
00:08:05,191 --> 00:08:06,633
So what else is there?

150
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:08,035
Station five.

151
00:08:08,035 --> 00:08:09,546
The real explanation.

152
00:08:09,546 --> 00:08:11,345
And it is not one answer.

153
00:08:11,345 --> 00:08:14,800
It is a stack of small ones, each with evidence.

154
00:08:15,266 --> 00:08:15,565
One.

155
00:08:15,565 --> 00:08:22,583
Real low frequency noise exists in the world, and some people are simply more sensitive to it.

156
00:08:22,583 --> 00:08:26,466
Traffic, pumps, wind turbines, even electrical gear.

157
00:08:26,933 --> 00:08:32,075
Low bass travels through walls and ground, and hardly fades with distance.

158
00:08:32,075 --> 00:08:36,799
A noise made miles away can appear in your bedroom and nowhere else.

159
00:08:37,266 --> 00:08:37,534
Two.

160
00:08:37,534 --> 00:08:40,212
In silence, the brain turns up its gain.

161
00:08:40,212 --> 00:08:44,899
A hum you could not hear at noon becomes loud at three in the morning.

162
00:08:45,366 --> 00:08:45,787
Three.

163
00:08:45,787 --> 00:08:47,189
Attention and worry.

164
00:08:47,189 --> 00:08:50,555
Once you notice a sound, you cannot unnotice it.

165
00:08:50,555 --> 00:08:53,430
The more you listen, the louder it seems.

166
00:08:53,430 --> 00:08:56,866
Researchers see this loop in many chronic sounds.

167
00:08:57,333 --> 00:08:57,686
Four.

168
00:08:57,686 --> 00:08:59,239
Stress and poor sleep.

169
00:08:59,239 --> 00:09:03,123
Many hearers were under pressure, and many slept badly.

170
00:09:03,123 --> 00:09:05,877
That does not make the sound imaginary.

171
00:09:05,877 --> 00:09:09,266
It makes it a real experience with a real cause.

172
00:09:09,733 --> 00:09:10,385
And five.

173
00:09:10,385 --> 00:09:11,255
Taos itself.

174
00:09:11,255 --> 00:09:17,346
A quiet town in a deep valley, where distant sounds carry and nights are very still.

175
00:09:17,346 --> 00:09:19,666
A perfect place to notice a hum.

176
00:09:20,133 --> 00:09:23,362
None of this proves the answer for every case.

177
00:09:23,362 --> 00:09:28,066
A few hearers may be picking up a real industrial source somewhere.

178
00:09:28,066 --> 00:09:31,366
The honest word is, mostly solved, partly open.

179
00:09:31,833 --> 00:09:38,591
Later studies elsewhere found the same pattern: hearers, no source, nothing on the instruments.

180
00:09:38,591 --> 00:09:42,433
The Taos hum became a model case in acoustic research.

181
00:09:42,899 --> 00:09:44,888
It also taught a hard lesson.

182
00:09:44,888 --> 00:09:49,481
When science finds nothing, that does not mean the person is lying.

183
00:09:49,481 --> 00:09:52,566
It can mean we are measuring the wrong thing.

184
00:09:53,033 --> 00:09:57,822
If you hear a hum yourself, you are not crazy, and you are not alone.

185
00:09:57,822 --> 00:10:03,166
A doctor and a quiet, steady sleep routine can help more than any conspiracy.

186
00:10:03,633 --> 00:10:05,702
So now we score every claim.

187
00:10:05,702 --> 00:10:08,805
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

188
00:10:08,805 --> 00:10:10,800
The Taos Hum, put on trial.

189
00:10:11,266 --> 00:10:13,732
One last thing before the scorecard.

190
00:10:13,732 --> 00:10:17,432
The most chilling part of the hum was never the sound.

191
00:10:17,432 --> 00:10:19,966
It was how alone it made people feel.

192
00:10:20,433 --> 00:10:20,966
Debrief.

193
00:10:20,966 --> 00:10:22,766
Time to score the Taos Hum.

194
00:10:23,233 --> 00:10:28,304
People report a real hum, hundreds of cases, in many places: evidence.

195
00:10:28,304 --> 00:10:31,999
No source found by a week of instruments: evidence.

196
00:10:31,999 --> 00:10:36,997
The same pattern in a survey, about two percent hearing it: evidence.

197
00:10:36,997 --> 00:10:39,533
Ear and brain explanations: likely.

198
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,398
Navy submarine radio: debunked as sound.

199
00:10:43,398 --> 00:10:44,757
HAARP: debunked.

200
00:10:44,757 --> 00:10:47,561
A secret alien base: no evidence.

201
00:10:47,561 --> 00:10:51,299
Industrial noise in some cases: partly open.

202
00:10:51,766 --> 00:10:52,674
The verdict.

203
00:10:52,674 --> 00:10:54,566
Not aliens, not a weapon.

204
00:10:54,566 --> 00:11:02,966
A real experience, from real ears and minds, probably helped along by distant low noise and a very quiet night.

205
00:11:02,966 --> 00:11:05,766
Mostly explained, never fully closed.

206
00:11:06,233 --> 00:11:09,353
And that may be the most human mystery of all.

207
00:11:09,353 --> 00:11:14,033
A sound that only some of us can hear, and that no machine can prove.

208
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:21,767
Next case: an ancient stone circle, a vanished builders' secret, and the strange sounds people say echo through it.

209
00:11:21,767 --> 00:11:23,600
What were they listening for?

210
00:11:24,066 --> 00:11:25,160
Do you hear a hum?

211
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:30,266
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
